					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aismallard" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4598089); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4598089&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200778" alt="aismallard" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4598089)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aismallard" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4598089); return false;" >aismallard</a></span> <span class="odate time_1546924865 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Jan 2019 05:21</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4112293">
									

<p>A really cool take on the &quot;compulsion effect&quot; cliche. I really like the ethical approach of attempting to cause as minimal damage from violence during the migration period, as well as the pragmatic realization that violence is <a href="/scp-3031">ultimately necessary</a>. +1</p>
<p>Though, I'm not a huge fan of the &quot;Ethics Committee as a GoI&quot; thing. I've held the view that the Foundation is strongest when its two goal-shaping organs, the O5 and EC, work <em>together</em> to secure, contain, and protect. I would even argue that some of the Foundation's greatest containment failures come from an <a href="/scp-1730">inability to be ethical enough</a>. Generally, I find scips that treat the EC as &quot;annoying intellectuals who get in the way of containment&quot; to not really understand the kind of consequentialist ethics the Foundation uses so well.</p>
<p>That said, I don't think that impacts the story much here, and the rest is so good, so it's an upvote from me.</p>

							</div>

								
					
